   │  │  
   │  │  ---
   │  │  
   │  │  ### issue found: redundant `then` in mutex journey t3
   │  │  
   │  │  `src/contract/index.mutex.journey.acceptance.test.ts:94-103`
   │  │  
   │  │  ```ts
   │  │  when('[t3] the holder releases via a fenced compare-and-delete', () => {
   │  │    then('the fenced release freed the lock', () => {
   │  │      expect(scene.afterRelease).toBeNull();           // ← this is the entire body
   │  │    });
   │  │  
   │  │    then('the post-release absent state is locked', () => {
   │  │      expect(scene.afterRelease).toBeNull();           // ← exact duplicate
   │  │      expect(scene.afterRelease).toMatchSnapshot();
   │  │    });
   │  │  });
   │  │  ```
   │  │  
   │  │  The first `then` is a strict subset of the second. It adds no independent behavioral claim — the null assertion is repeated, and the snapshot in the second block already locks that null. This violates the one-claim-per-then principle and creates reader confusion about what distinct thing is being verified.
   │  │  
   │  │  **Fix**: remove the first `then` block entirely. The second covers both the assertion and the snapshot.
   │  │  
   │  │  ```ts
   │  │  when('[t3] the holder releases via a fenced compare-and-delete', () => {
   │  │    then('the fenced release freed the lock (null + snapped)', () => {
   │  │      expect(scene.afterRelease).toBeNull();
   │  │      expect(scene.afterRelease).toMatchSnapshot();
   │  │    });
   │  │  });
   │  │  ```
   │  │  
   │  │  Or split into genuinely distinct claims:
   │  │  
   │  │  ```ts
   │  │  when('[t3] the holder releases via a fenced compare-and-delete', () => {
   │  │    then('the lock is absent', () => {
   │  │      expect(scene.afterRelease).toBeNull();
   │  │    });
   │  │  
   │  │    then('the post-release absent state is locked', () => {
   │  │      expect(scene.afterRelease).toMatchSnapshot();
   │  │    });
   │  │  });
   │  │  ```
   │  │  
   │  │  ---
   │  │  
   │  │  ### verdict
   │  │  
   │  │  Snapshot coverage: **✅ strong** — all return-value shapes are snapped with appropriate property matchers on live values (ETags). The `get.all` partial snap and `set` overwrite void result are both defensible.
   │  │  
   │  │  Journey coverage: **✅ complete** — base CRUD, URI variants, error paths, and mutex lifecycle are all present.
   │  │  
   │  │  One **nitpick**: redundant `then` in mutex journey t3 (lines 94-103). Not a functional gap but a clean test structure issue.
